  <body class="home">
{{> header}}
    <main class="home-main">

      <section class="hero">
        <p class="hero-eyebrow">Alpha · single-user · tested on a DGX Spark and an RTX 3090 workstation</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title">The setup a platform team would give you.<br><span class="accent">If you had one.</span></h1>
        <p class="hero-sub">Your machine stops breaking. Every experiment is findable. Every project
          starts the same way, and there is a path from a notebook to something with an address — a
          URL, a certificate, a login in front of it. You never have to learn Kubernetes, identity,
          registries or GPU scheduling to get there.</p>
        <div class="hero-cta">
          <a class="btn btn-primary" href="start-here/what-is-thinkube.html">What is Thinkube?</a>
          <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="../install/overview.html">Before you install</a>
        </div>

        <ul class="facts" aria-label="What is different on a machine running Thinkube">
          <li class="fact"><strong>Environments are built from definitions, not remembered.</strong>
            Install one more package and what worked yesterday still works.</li>
          <li class="fact"><strong>Models are downloaded once</strong>, stored, versioned, and served
            from where they are. It says what fits on your hardware before you download.</li>
          <li class="fact"><strong>Experiment tracking is already running</strong> and already logged
            in. A run you cannot find did not happen; here you can find it.</li>
          <li class="fact"><strong>A template, a git repository, an image, an address.</strong>
            What you built stops being a notebook.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="plain">
        <span class="kicker">What comes out</span>
        <h2 class="section-h">A git repository, not a configuration</h2>
        <p class="plain-lead">Not a no-code platform. The easy part is the same — describe what you
          want. What comes out is real code: source, a Dockerfile, tests, and about twenty lines
          describing ports and routes, built into a standard image, running on certified Kubernetes.
          Delete Thinkube tomorrow and that repository still builds and still deploys anywhere.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="cockpit">
        <div class="cockpit-copy">
          <span class="kicker">How you work</span>
          <h2 class="section-h">You can run it from Claude Code</h2>
          <p class="cockpit-lead">Thinkube IDE comes with Claude Code, and Claude Code can see the
            platform: which models are loaded, what the GPU is holding, what a deploy is doing, what a
            service logged. Ask it to deploy a template or load a model and it uses the same operations
            the control panel uses — about a hundred of them. Nothing changes unless you ask. Everything
            Claude can do here, you can also click in thinkube-control.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="codewin" role="img"
             aria-label="A Claude Code session: deploy a template and load a model, driven by MCP tools.">
          <div class="codewin-bar">
            <span class="codewin-dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
            <span class="codewin-title">Thinkube IDE — claude</span>
          </div>
<pre class="codewin-body" aria-hidden="true"><code><span class="c-prompt">›</span> <span class="c-user">deploy the vLLM template and load Qwen3.5-4B</span>

<span class="c-claude">●</span> Deploying tkt-vllm-gradio.
  <span class="c-ok">✓</span> <span class="c-tool">deploy_template</span><span class="c-paren">(</span>tkt-vllm-gradio<span class="c-paren">)</span>       <span class="c-dim">→ pending</span>
  <span class="c-ok">✓</span> <span class="c-tool">get_deployment_status</span><span class="c-paren">(</span>…<span class="c-paren">)</span>              <span class="c-dim">→ building → running</span>

<span class="c-claude">●</span> Loading the model.
  <span class="c-ok">✓</span> <span class="c-tool">load_llm_model</span><span class="c-paren">(</span>Qwen/Qwen3.5-4B<span class="c-paren">)</span>         <span class="c-dim">→ loading · backend: vllm</span>
  <span class="c-ok">✓</span> <span class="c-tool">get_llm_model_status</span><span class="c-paren">(</span>…<span class="c-paren">)</span>               <span class="c-dim">→ available</span>

  Your app is at <span class="c-link">vllm-gradio.thinkube.com</span></code></pre>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="plain alpha">
        <span class="kicker">Alpha</span>
        <h2 class="section-h">Two machines have run this. Yours will be the third kind.</h2>
        <p class="plain-lead">Tested on a DGX Spark (GB10, arm64) and an x86-64 workstation with
          RTX 3090s, both on Ubuntu 24.04. A future version may require reinstalling from scratch;
          there is no promised migration path. Install it if you have a machine you can afford to
          reinstall, and you would write down what happened.</p>
      </section>

